{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VKDn6koiccBokH2M7f3mwV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd003-format\n2\ttitle: \"pkg/format: Output Formatting\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n6\t  fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n7\t  file types, and both ls and du convert raw byte counts to human-readable sizes\n8\t  (4.0K, 128M, 1.5G). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own\n9\t  column width computation, ANSI escape handling, and unit conversion, producing\n10\t  inconsistent behavior across utilities and making differential test normalization\n11\t  harder.\n12\t\n13\t  The formatting logic is driven by C source requirements in ls.c and du.c. Each\n14\t  capability is listed with its utility context so the API reflects real call sites,\n15\t  not speculation.\n16\t\n17\tgoals:\n18\t  - G1: Provide a column-width computation and padding function for multi-column tabular output, matching ls column-layout behavior.\n19\t  - G2: Provide an ANSI color lookup and output function for file-type colorization, with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n20\t  - G3: Provide a human-readable size conversion function matching GNU coreutils human_readable() behavior for both ls -h and du -h.\n21\t\n22\trequirements:\n23\t  R1:\n24\t    title: Column alignment\n25\t    items:\n26\t      - R1.1:\n27\t          text: Must provide a Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, using the longest entry in each column to determine column width.\n28\t      - R1.2:\n29\t          text: Must provide a PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string function for left- and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column.\n30\t      - R1.3:\n31\t          text: 'Must treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly: column width must be computed from display width (rune count via utf8.RuneCountInString), not byte count. East-asian full-width character handling is deferred; rune count is sufficient for ASCII-dominant utility output in the current roadmap.'\n32\t      - R1.4:\n33\t          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c multi-column layout): ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.'\n34\t\n35\t  R2:\n36\t    title: ANSI color\n37\t    items:\n38\t      - R2.1:\n39\t          text: Must provide a FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function that returns the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device, socket, pipe, regular file).\n40\t      - R2.2:\n41\t          text: Must provide a Reset() string constant or function that returns the ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\").\n42\t      - R2.3:\n43\t          text: |\n44\t            Must provide a ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function that returns true only when w is a TTY. Implementation: attempt a type assertion to *os.File; if it fails return false; call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(f.Fd()) which returns true when the file descriptor is a terminal (detected via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl), and false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors. When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string.\n45\t      - R2.4:\n46\t          text: 'Must use the same color assignments as GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), character device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (reset/default).'\n47\t      - R2.5:\n48\t          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h, ls.c:273 get_color_indicator): ls outputs ANSI escapes before and after each filename in colorized mode. Color is suppressed when stdout is not a TTY or when --color=never is set.'\n49\t      - R2.6:\n50\t          text: |\n51\t            Must provide func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) that overrides the automatic TTY\n52\t            detection from ColorEnabled. When set to true, FileTypeColor and Reset return ANSI\n53\t            sequences regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. When set to false, they return\n54\t            empty strings regardless. This supports --color=always and --color=never flags in\n55\t            utilities like ls. The override is process-global.\n56\t      - R2.7:\n57\t          text: |\n58\t            Must provide func ResetColorEnabled() that clears any override set by\n59\t            SetColorEnabled, reverting to automatic TTY detection via ColorEnabled. Utilities\n60\t            must call ResetColorEnabled in deferred cleanup or test teardown to avoid leaking\n61\t            the override across test cases.\n62\t\n63\t  R3:\n64\t    title: Human-readable size conversion\n65\t    items:\n66\t      - R3.1:\n67\t          text: |\n68\t            Must provide func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts is defined as:\n69\t\n70\t              type HumanSizeOpts struct {\n71\t                  Binary bool // true = 1024-based with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E (default); false = SI 1000-based with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB\n72\t              }\n73\t      - R3.2:\n74\t          text: |\n75\t            When opts.Binary is true (the default zero value), use base 1024 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}. When opts.Binary is false, use base 1000 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"kB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"}.\n76\t      - R3.3:\n77\t          text: Must format the output to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes must produce \"1.5K\", not \"2K\").\n78\t      - R3.4:\n79\t          text: Must return \"0\" for a zero byte count regardless of the unit mode.\n80\t      - R3.5:\n81\t          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c -h flag, human_output_opts): ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output. The same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts).'\n82\t      - R3.6:\n83\t          text: 'Utility context — du (du.c:153 human_output_opts, du.c:442 human_readable()): du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings. du uses the same binary/SI distinction as ls -h.'\n84\t\n85\tnon_goals:\n86\t  - pkg/format does not read or parse LS_COLORS environment variables; it uses GNU ls defaults only.\n87\t  - pkg/format does not implement terminal hyperlinks (ls.c:210 mentions them as a feature but they are not in scope).\n88\t  - pkg/format does not provide a full table library; it provides only the column-alignment primitives needed by ls and du.\n89\t  - pkg/format does not handle color themes beyond the GNU ls defaults.\n90\t\n91\tpackage_contract:\n92\t  exports:\n93\t    - name: HumanSizeOpts\n94\t      signature: \"type HumanSizeOpts struct { Binary bool }\"\n95\t    - name: HumanSize\n96\t      signature: \"func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string\"\n97\t    - name: FileTypeColor\n98\t      signature: \"func FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string\"\n99\t    - name: Reset\n100\t      signature: \"func Reset() string\"\n101\t    - name: ColorEnabled\n102\t      signature: \"func ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool\"\n103\t    - name: SetColorEnabled\n104\t      signature: \"func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool)\"\n105\t    - name: ResetColorEnabled\n106\t      signature: \"func ResetColorEnabled()\"\n107\t    - name: PadRight\n108\t      signature: \"func PadRight(s string, width int) string\"\n109\t    - name: PadLeft\n110\t      signature: \"func PadLeft(s string, width int) string\"\n111\t    - name: Columns\n112\t      signature: \"func Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string\"\n113\t  imports:\n114\t    - io\n115\t    - os\n116\t    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n117\t  import_constraints:\n118\t    - \"Must not import any cmd/ package.\"\n119\t\n120\tdepends_on:\n121\t  - srd_id: srd002-sys\n122\t    symbols_used:\n123\t      - IsTerminal\n124\t\n125\tacceptance_criteria:\n126\t  - id: AC1\n127\t    criterion: \"Columns(entries, termWidth) with a list of 20 filenames and termWidth=80 produces the same column layout as gls --format=across for the same input.\"\n128\t    traces:\n129\t      - R1.1\n130\t      - R1.2\n131\t      - R1.3\n132\t      - R1.4\n133\t  - id: AC2\n134\t    criterion: \"FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for each of the eight file types listed in R2.4.\"\n135\t    traces:\n136\t      - R2.4\n137\t  - id: AC3\n138\t    criterion: \"ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY), and true when passed os.Stdout in a TTY test.\"\n139\t    traces:\n140\t      - R1.1\n141\t      - R1.2\n142\t      - R1.3\n143\t      - R1.4\n144\t  - id: AC4\n145\t    criterion: \"HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \\\"0\\\".\"\n146\t    traces:\n147\t      - R1.1\n148\t      - R1.2\n149\t      - R1.3\n150\t      - R1.4\n151\t  - id: AC5\n152\t    criterion: \"SetColorEnabled(true) causes FileTypeColor to return ANSI codes even when stdout is a pipe. SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor to return empty strings even when stdout is a TTY. ResetColorEnabled reverts to automatic detection.\"\n153\t    traces:\n154\t      - R2.3\n155\t      - R2.6\n156\t  - id: AC6\n157\t    criterion: \"All pkg/format code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n158\t    traces:\n159\t      - R1.1\n160\t      - R1.2\n161\t      - R1.3\n162\t      - R1.4\n163\t      - R2.1\n164\t      - R2.2\n165\t      - R2.3\n166\t      - R2.4\n167\t      - R2.5\n168\t      - R2.6\n169\t      - R2.7\n170\t      - R3.1\n171\t      - R3.2\n172\t      - R3.3\n173\t      - R3.4\n174\t      - R3.5\n175\t      - R3.6\n176\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95bece5e-aa9b-42f1-8065-fcb773f53fe0","uuid":"0b4cf83c-71fe-4475-8f9e-cb3488cfc58a","timestamp":"2026-05-13T23:03:24.669Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5087/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd003-format.yaml","content":"id: srd003-format\ntitle: \"pkg/format: Output Formatting\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n  fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n  file types, and both ls and du convert raw byte counts to human-readable sizes\n  (4.0K, 128M, 1.5G). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own\n  column width computation, ANSI escape handling, and unit conversion, producing\n  inconsistent behavior across utilities and making differential test normalization\n  harder.\n\n  The formatting logic is driven by C source requirements in ls.c and du.c. Each\n  capability is listed with its utility context so the API reflects real call sites,\n  not speculation.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a column-width computation and padding function for multi-column tabular output, matching ls column-layout behavior.\n  - G2: Provide an ANSI color lookup and output function for file-type colorization, with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n  - G3: Provide a human-readable size conversion function matching GNU coreutils human_readable() behavior for both ls -h and du -h.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Column alignment\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: Must provide a Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, using the longest entry in each column to determine column width.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: Must provide a PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string function for left- and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: 'Must treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly: column width must be computed from display width (rune count via utf8.RuneCountInString), not byte count. East-asian full-width character handling is deferred; rune count is sufficient for ASCII-dominant utility output in the current roadmap.'\n      - R1.4:\n          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c multi-column layout): ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: ANSI color\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: Must provide a FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function that returns the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device, socket, pipe, regular file).\n      - R2.2:\n          text: Must provide a Reset() string constant or function that returns the ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\").\n      - R2.3:\n          text: |\n            Must provide a ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function that returns true only when w is a TTY. Implementation: attempt a type assertion to *os.File; if it fails return false; call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(f.Fd()) which returns true when the file descriptor is a terminal (detected via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl), and false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors. When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string.\n      - R2.4:\n          text: 'Must use the same color assignments as GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), character device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (reset/default).'\n      - R2.5:\n          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h, ls.c:273 get_color_indicator): ls outputs ANSI escapes before and after each filename in colorized mode. Color is suppressed when stdout is not a TTY or when --color=never is set.'\n      - R2.6:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) that overrides the automatic TTY\n            detection from ColorEnabled. When set to true, FileTypeColor and Reset return ANSI\n            sequences regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. When set to false, they return\n            empty strings regardless. This supports --color=always and --color=never flags in\n            utilities like ls. The override is process-global.\n      - R2.7:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func ResetColorEnabled() that clears any override set by\n            SetColorEnabled, reverting to automatic TTY detection via ColorEnabled. Utilities\n            must call ResetColorEnabled in deferred cleanup or test teardown to avoid leaking\n            the override across test cases.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Human-readable size conversion\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts is defined as:\n\n              type HumanSizeOpts struct {\n                  Binary bool // true = 1024-based with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E (default); false = SI 1000-based with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB\n              }\n      - R3.2:\n          text: |\n            When opts.Binary is true (the default zero value), use base 1024 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}. When opts.Binary is false, use base 1000 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"kB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"}.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: Must format the output to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes must produce \"1.5K\", not \"2K\").\n      - R3.4:\n          text: Must return \"0\" for a zero byte count regardless of the unit mode.\n      - R3.5:\n          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c -h flag, human_output_opts): ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output. The same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts).'\n      - R3.6:\n          text: 'Utility context — du (du.c:153 human_output_opts, du.c:442 human_readable()): du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings. du uses the same binary/SI distinction as ls -h.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/format does not read or parse LS_COLORS environment variables; it uses GNU ls defaults only.\n  - pkg/format does not implement terminal hyperlinks (ls.c:210 mentions them as a feature but they are not in scope).\n  - pkg/format does not provide a full table library; it provides only the column-alignment primitives needed by ls and du.\n  - pkg/format does not handle color themes beyond the GNU ls defaults.\n\npackage_contract:\n  exports:\n    - name: HumanSizeOpts\n      signature: \"type HumanSizeOpts struct { Binary bool }\"\n    - name: HumanSize\n      signature: \"func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string\"\n    - name: FileTypeColor\n      signature: \"func FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string\"\n    - name: Reset\n      signature: \"func Reset() string\"\n    - name: ColorEnabled\n      signature: \"func ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool\"\n    - name: SetColorEnabled\n      signature: \"func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool)\"\n    - name: ResetColorEnabled\n      signature: \"func ResetColorEnabled()\"\n    - name: PadRight\n      signature: \"func PadRight(s string, width int) string\"\n    - name: PadLeft\n      signature: \"func PadLeft(s string, width int) string\"\n    - name: Columns\n      signature: \"func Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string\"\n  imports:\n    - io\n    - os\n    - github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys\n  import_constraints:\n    - \"Must not import any cmd/ package.\"\n\ndepends_on:\n  - srd_id: srd002-sys\n    symbols_used:\n      - IsTerminal\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"Columns(entries, termWidth) with a list of 20 filenames and termWidth=80 produces the same column layout as gls --format=across for the same input.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for each of the eight file types listed in R2.4.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.4\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY), and true when passed os.Stdout in a TTY test.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \\\"0\\\".\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"SetColorEnabled(true) causes FileTypeColor to return ANSI codes even when stdout is a pipe. SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor to return empty strings even when stdout is a TTY. ResetColorEnabled reverts to automatic detection.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.6\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"All pkg/format code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n      - R2.5\n      - R2.6\n      - R2.7\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R3.5\n      - R3.6\n","numLines":176,"startLine":1,"totalLines":176}}}
